,50,3948,279,30169,84,763,400,17,77,1124,57317,220,16,20,1124,79,2593,90,19,22,31716,11,438,264,48132,59864,220,19,22,13,220,320,35127,458,4226,1948,220,15,323,220,19,21,6138,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSolve the congruence $2n \\equiv 15 \\pmod{47}$, as a residue modulo 47.  (Give an answer between 0 and 46.)","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"65b208af07708e3512e658b4a86ad6c3c89e88365b225ad36c46d1cf2e3aa23b"},"seconds":12.648579120635986,"seed":821074089554348197,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the congruence \\(2n \\equiv 15 \\pmod{47}\\), we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 47. This means we need to find an integer \\(x\\) such that \\(2x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{47}\\).\n\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. We start by applying the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 2 and 47:\n\n\\[\n47 = 2 \\cdot 23 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n2 = 1 \\cdot 2 + 0\n\\]\n\nThe gcd is 1, and we can backtrack to express 1 as a combination of 2 and 47:\n\n\\[\n1 = 47 - 2 \\cdot 23\n\\]\n\nThis tells us that \\( -23 \\) is a solution to \\(2x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{47}\\). Since we want a positive residue, we convert \\(-23\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 47:\n\n\\[\n-23 + 47 = 24\n\\]\n\nSo, the multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 47 is 24. Now we can solve for \\(n\\) by multiplying both sides of the original congruence \\(2n \\equiv 15 \\pmod{47}\\) by 24:\n\n\\[\n24 \\cdot 2n \\equiv 24 \\cdot 15 \\pmod{47}\n\\]\n\\[\n48n \\equiv 360 \\pmod{47}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(48 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{47}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nn \\equiv 360 \\pmod{47}\n\\]\n\nNext, we reduce 360 modulo 47:\n\n\\[\n360 \\div 47 = 7 \\text{ remainder } 31\n\\]\n\\[\n360 = 47 \\cdot 7 + 31\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n360 \\equiv 31 \\pmod{47}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution to the congruence \\(2n \\equiv 15 \\pmod{47}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{31}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{31}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:532"}
